%I A398670 #35 Aug 22 2026 15:21:50
%S A398670 1,5,49,39,0,275,0,455,1643524,19375,0,2975,0,403136,16623460624,8855,
%T A398670 0,94275,0,25839,296002841789890624,1185172479,0,66975
%N A398670 a(n) is the smallest number m with n divisors such that m+1 has 2*n divisors; or 0 if no such number exists.
%C A398670 From _Pontus von Brömssen_, Aug 06 2026: (Start)
%C A398670 Proof that a(5) = 0: For m and m+1 to have the right number of divisors, we must have m = p^4 and either m+1 = q^9 or m+1 = q*r^4 for distinct primes p, q, and r. The case m+1 = q^9 is easily ruled out as is the case p = 2. So m+1 = p^4+1 is even and either q or r must be 2. If r = 2, p^4+1 = 16*q, which is impossible since the square of the odd number p^2 is congruent to 1 mod 4. So we must have q = 2 and p^4+1 = 2*r^4, which we write as (p^2)^2+1 = 2*r^4. By Ljunggren's theorem, the only possibilities are p^2 = 1 and p^2 = 239 (see A229384), but neither 1 nor 239 is the square of a prime.
%C A398670 A similar proof shows that a(n) = 0 when n is a prime congruent to 1 mod 4.
%C A398670 Proof that a(7) = 0: In this case we must have m = p^6 and either m+1 = q^13 or m+1 = q*r^6 for distinct primes p, q, and r. As above, the cases m+1 = q^13 and p = 2 are easily ruled out, so either q or r must be 2. If r = 2, p^6+1 = 64*q, which is impossible since the square of the odd number p^3 is congruent to 1 mod 4. So we must have q = 2 and 2*r^6 = p^6+1 = (p^2+1)*(p^4-p^2+1). Since p is odd, so is p^4-p^2+1 and we must have p^4-p^2+1 = r^k and p^2+1 = 2*r^(6-k) where 0 <= k <= 6. k = 0 and k = 6 are both impossible since p^4-p^2+1 > 1 and p^2+1 > 2. Since p^4-p^2+1 = (p^2+1)*(p^2-2)+3, the only possible common prime factor of p^4-p^2+1 and p^2+1 is 3, so r = 3 and we get p^6+1 = 2*3^6 which is impossible.
%C A398670 Similarly, it can be proved that when n > 3 is a prime congruent to 3 mod 4, we must have m+1 = 2*r^(n-1) = p^(n-1)+1 = (p^2+1)*(p^(n-3)-p^(n-5)+...+1), and p^(n-3)-p^(n-5)+...+1 = (p^2+1)*(p^(n-5)-2*p^(n-7)+3*p^(n-9)-...-(n-3)/2)+(n-1)/2 shows that r must be a divisor of (n-1)/2, so there are only a finite number of cases to check. For n = 11, for example, we must have r = 5 and 2*5^10 = p^10+1 which is impossible, so a(11) = 0; and for n = 19 we must have r = 3 and 2*3^18 = p^18+1, which is impossible, so a(19) = 0. (End)
%C A398670 a(26) = 12371415039, a(27) = 48225038404, a(28) = 2642624, a(29) = 0, a(30) = 1739375, a(31) = 0, a(32)=156519. - _Pontus von Brömssen_, Aug 16 2026
%C A398670 a(25) <= 8948508033158^4, a(33) = 514912633128212890624. - _Jinyuan Wang_, Aug 22 2026
%D A398670 Wilhelm Ljunggren, Zur Theorie der Gleichung x^2+1 = Dy^4, Avh. Norske Vid. Akad. Oslo I(5) (1942), 1-27.
%e A398670 39 is the smallest number with 4 divisors such that 39+1 has 8 divisors.
